/// @file modules.dox
/// @brief Doxygen module group definitions for the QPlayer API reference
///
///
/// Documentation-only input: no C++ declarations live here, so nothing needs a
/// CMake target_sources entry. Each public header joins exactly one group with
/// an \@ingroup tag.
///
/// Group boundaries follow the existing source directory structure rather than
/// introducing a second classification axis.

/// @defgroup api User-facing API
/// @brief The surface a QPlayer program calls directly.
///
/// QRegister is the sole entry point: it owns a simulator backend and drives both
/// Batch execution (parse, transpile, run) and Interactive immediate dispatch.
/// Circuit builds the program, Result carries what a run produced, and Observable
/// with the Sampler / Estimator facades cover the Qiskit-compatible primitives.

/// @defgroup ir Circuit IR
/// @brief The op stream a Circuit holds and every pass rewrites.
///
/// Op is a variant over gate, measure, barrier, conditional, reset and idle
/// nodes. Parameter carries symbolic gate arguments and CircuitMetadata carries
/// circuit-level annotations such as the qubit layout produced by routing.

/// @defgroup frontend OpenQASM v2 front end
/// @brief Turns QASM source text into a Circuit.
///
/// Lexer, parser, semantic validator and circuit builder are self-implemented; no
/// external parser generator is used. Only the Parser facade is public — the
/// stages live under parser/detail and stay out of this reference.

/// @defgroup transpiler Transpiler and passes
/// @brief Rewrites a circuit before execution.
///
/// Transpiler is the single compile entry point. It assembles a PassManager from
/// the requested optimisation level and the bound device, then runs the pipeline:
/// validation, optimisation, layout, routing, translation, analysis and
/// scheduling. Every stage is an IPass, so adding one means adding a class here.

/// @defgroup backend Simulator backends
/// @brief Executes a compiled circuit and holds the quantum state.
///
/// SimulatorBackend is the interface each family implements: FullState (dense
/// statevector on CPU or GPU), SparseState (hash-map amplitudes for circuits with
/// few non-zero terms) and TensorNetwork (MPS and general contraction). Supporting
/// machinery — SIMD dispatch, gate fusion, Kraus application, memory budgeting —
/// sits alongside them.

/// @defgroup noise Noise and device models
/// @brief Describes hardware imperfection and synthesises the channels for it.
///
/// DeviceModel is the hardware characterisation (T1/T2, per-gate duration and
/// error, readout confusion, coupling map). NoiseModel is the registry of Kraus
/// channels a noisy run applies; load_device() converts a characterisation into
/// channels keyed by the device's native basis gates.

/// @defgroup core Core types and diagnostics
/// @brief Vocabulary types shared by every layer.
///
/// The four index aliases (QubitIndex, ClassicalBitIndex, ClassicalRegisterIndex,
/// StateIndex) are centrally defined so a qubit number can never be confused with
/// a state-space subscript. The exception hierarchy, logging and timing helpers
/// round the group out.

/// @example run_qasm.cpp
/// End-to-end command-line driver: parse a .qasm file, transpile it, run it for a
/// requested shot count and print the measurement counts. The shortest complete
/// path through the Batch API.

/// @example noise_demo.cpp
/// Device-noise run: load a hardware characterisation, execute the same circuit
/// with and without noise, and compare the resulting distributions.
